  "title": "NATO Pledges to Defend Poland. Why Weren’t the Russian Objects Shot Down?",
  "deck": "Poland identified one of three objects as a Russian cruise missile. NATO scrambled jets, and Poland’s operational commander said the conditions for firing were never met.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T16:11:46.639Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-21T16:11:46.639Z",
  "body": [
    "Three objects from Russia crossed into Polish airspace overnight into Friday, November 20, during a wider Russian attack on Ukraine. Polish Defense Minister Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz identified one as a Kh-101 cruise missile. He described the other two as a suspected missile and a likely jet-powered drone. The objects came down in southeastern Poland. No injuries were reported.",
    "NATO scrambled jets in response. The next day in Warsaw, NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte pledged to defend every inch of Polish territory. The two events raise a practical question for the alliance. When a threat crosses into allied airspace, what determines whether the forces sent to respond can shoot it down?",
    "## Why the jets didn’t fire",
    "Polish operational commander Lt. Gen. Ireneusz Nowak said Swedish pilots attempted to intercept what was likely the Kh-101. He said difficult weather prevented them from identifying the target visually or with onboard optical sensors. No weapons were fired because the conditions for using them were not met, Nowak said.",
    "That account gives a specific explanation for why the attempted interception ended without a shot. It also gives Warsaw a concrete issue to raise with allies. Can they improve the ability of crews to identify incoming threats in conditions like those on Friday?",
    "## Why the timing drew attention",
    "The incursion came as troops from 11 European countries were taking part in an exercise at Nowa Dęba, Poland, to prepare for a possible security mission in Ukraine after a peace settlement. The objects came down in the same region as the exercise.",
    "Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk said he believed the airspace violation was a deliberate provocation timed to coincide with the drill. The Institute for the Study of War assessed that Moscow could have been testing NATO’s response and trying to discourage European involvement in Ukraine. Russia was also carrying out a wider attack on Ukraine that night, so the timing alone leaves Moscow’s intent unresolved.",
    "## What NATO’s pledge means in practice",
    "Article 5 of the North Atlantic Treaty treats an armed attack on one ally as an attack on all. It obliges each ally to assist through measures it deems necessary, which can include armed force. NATO assesses what qualifies as an armed attack case by case. The decision to engage an object in Polish airspace is an operational decision that crews and commanders may have to make during the incident itself.",
    "Poland’s defense minister asked allies for additional airborne surveillance and F-35 fighter aircraft. Rutte said NATO’s top commander in Europe would assess threats to the alliance’s defenses and make adjustments where needed, according to the Polish Press Agency.",
    "## What happens next",
    "The Polish Press Agency reported that NATO’s North Atlantic Council is scheduled to discuss the incursion in Brussels on Monday, November 23. For Warsaw, the question is whether that meeting leads to additional aircraft or other changes that help crews identify and respond to threats over Poland."
  ],
